iner"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S2.F2.pic1.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{3}^{(2)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mn>3</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{3}^{(2)}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g stroke-width="2.84528pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 387.4 -179.53 M 387.4 -179.53 L 387.4 -135.43 L 740.16 -135.43 L 740.16 -179.53 Z M 740.16 -135.43"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 524.98 -117.45)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:3.61em;--ltx-fo-height:0.7em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.2em;font-size:11.75pt;" height="14.58" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 11.35)" width="58.71"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="S2.F2.pic1.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:120%;">Memory</span></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 451.68 -205.04)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:12.43em;--ltx-fo-height:0.69em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.19em;font-size:10pt;" height="12.3" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.61)" width="171.93"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="S2.F2.pic1.6" class="ltx_text">Max <math id="S2.F2.pic1.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=8</annotation></semantics></math> experts loaded at once</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></g></g></svg></span>
</span></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S2.F2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 2</span>: </span><span id="S2.F2.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Example of experts used in the production of three tokens. The
models has <math id="S2.F2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell=4</annotation></semantics></math> layers, each with <math id="S2.F2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=4</annotation></semantics></math> experts. The experts
used for each token are represented by a different color. Expert <math id="S2.F2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{2}^{(3)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{2}^{(3)}</annotation></semantics></math>
is used for token 1 and reused for 2, hence resulting in a cache
hit. The figure on the right depicts the state of the cache when processing the third layer for token 3: Expert <math id="S2.F2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{4}^{(3)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mn>4</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{4}^{(3)}</annotation></semantics></math> is
requested, but the memory is already full, hence some other expert
needs to be evicted.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2 </span>Layered Paging</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We now proceed with the formal definition of our problem, which captures the setting discussed in the previous section. We say that the data produced by each expert corresponds to a <span id="S2.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">page</span>, and recall that the cache has a capacity <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, in that it can store <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> pages. There are <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\ell</annotation></semantics></math> possible pages in total, given that each of the <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> experts may produce a page in each of the <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math> layers. The set of pages <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> can be thus decomposed in <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math> disjoint sets <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{1},\ldots L_{\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{1},\ldots L_{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math>, each of cardinality <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>. We denote the pages inside each set <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{p_{1}^{(i)},p_{2}^{(i)},\dots,p_{n}^{(i)}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{p_{1}^{(i)},p_{2}^{(i)},\dots,p_{n}^{(i)}\}</annotation></semantics></math>. The input <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> to our problem is a sequence of pages <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma=p_{1},p_{2},\ldots" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma=p_{1},p_{2},\ldots</annotation></semantics></math>, with the constraint that
<math id="S2.SS2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{i}\in L_{(i-1\bmod\ell)+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>mod</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{i}\in L_{(i-1\bmod\ell)+1}</annotation></semantics></math>, modeling the layered structure of experts in the MoE architecture. For the purpose of analysis, a <span id="S2.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">round</span> in <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as a consecutive block of pages of the form <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{j},\ldots,p_{j+\ell-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{j},\ldots,p_{j+\ell-1}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in[j,j+\ell-1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in[j,j+\ell-1]</annotation></semantics></math> belongs to layer <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{i-j+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{i-j+1}</annotation></semantics></math>, so <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math> divides <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j-1</annotation></semantics></math>. For an illustration, each round is depicted in Figure <a href="#S2.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 2.1 Caching in MoE Architectures ‣ 2 Layered Paging: a Model for MoE Caching in LLMs ‣ Cache Management for Mixture-of-Experts LLMs – extended version" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> by its proper color.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Having defined the set of pages and the structure of an input sequence, the optimization objective is as in the standard online paging. Namely, if a requested page is present in the cache, which corresponds to a cache <span id="S2.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">hit</span>, then the page is served at zero cost. In contrast, if a requested page is not in the cache, which corresponds to a cache <span id="S2.SS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">miss</span>, then the algorithm must determine which page to evict from the cache, in order to bring in the requested page. In this case, the page is served at cost equal to 1. Note that any paging algorithm can be fully described as a cache eviction policy, which determines the page to be evicted in the event of a cache miss.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We will refer to the above problem as the <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">-layer paging</span> problem, or <span id="S2.SS2.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">layered paging</span>, for simplicity. Note that this problem is a generalization of standard online paging, since the latter is equivalent to <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>-layered paging. To analyze the performance of algorithms for the problem, we will rely on the canonical framework of competitive analysis. Let <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> denote an online paging algorithm, and <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A(\sigma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A(\sigma)</annotation></semantics></math> its <span id="S2.SS2.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">cost</span> on input <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e., the total number of cache misses it incurs on <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math>. Let also <span id="S2.SS2.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Opt</span> denote an optimal <span id="S2.SS2.p3.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">offline</span> algorithm that has foreknowledge of <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math>. It is known that an optimal offline algorithm is the one that, upon each request that results in a cache miss, evicts a page that will be requested the furthest in the future <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmdefinition1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmdefinition1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 1</span></span><span id="Thmdefinition1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> (Competitive Ratio)</span></h6>
<div id="Thmdefinition1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmdefinition1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A deterministic algorithm <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> is
<math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>-competitive if there is a constant <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c\in\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c\in\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> such that for any request sequence <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math>, it holds that</p>
<table id="S2.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A(\sigma)\leqslant r\cdot{\textsc{Opt}}(\sigma)+c." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⩽</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Opt</mtext></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A(\sigma)\leqslant r\cdot{\textsc{Opt}}(\sigma)+c.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmdefinition1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Similarly, a randomized algorithm <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>-competitive if for every request <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math></p>
<table id="S2.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathds{E}(A(\sigma))\leqslant r\cdot{\textsc{Opt}}(\sigma)+c," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⩽</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Opt</mtext></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathds{E}(A(\sigma))\leqslant r\cdot{\textsc{Opt}}(\sigma)+c,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmdefinition1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">where the expectation is over the random choices of <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We will denote by <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\CR(A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">C</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\CR(A)</annotation></semantics></math> the competitive ratio of an algorithm <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The paging problem was often studied in the more general setting of the
<math id="S2.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-server problem <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>, <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite>. The book <span id="S2.SS2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">"Randomized Algorithms"</span> by
<span id="S2.SS2.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Motwani</span> &amp; <span id="S2.SS2.p5.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Raghavan</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite>
conveys a lot of the intuition behind the study of randomized
algorithms, with a section focusing on the paging problem, proving
some previously mentioned results.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Competitive Analysis of Layered Paging</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we present theoretical results on the competitive ratio of deterministic and randomized algorithms for layered paging.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Deterministic Algorithms</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A first approach is to consider an online algorithm that devotes a predetermined portion of the cache to requests of each layer; <span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">e.g.</span>, a portion of size approximately equal to <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k/\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>/</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k/\ell</annotation></semantics></math> to each layer. While such simple approaches are used in practical implementations <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>, we show that they result in poor theoretical performance. Fixed cache partitions lead to inefficiencies, aligning with prior results on parallel paging <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 3.1</span></span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Any deterministic algorithm that allocates a fixed portion of the cache
to a requests of a given layer has unbounded competitive ratio, as long as <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geqslant 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>⩾</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geqslant 2</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell\geqslant 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⩾</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell\geqslant 2</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmproofx1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmproofx1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof</span></span></h6>
<div id="Thmproofx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmproofx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We consider a layered paging instance and an algorithm <math id="Thmproofx1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> with a fixed cache allocation per layer. Let the total cache size <math id="Thmproofx1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> be such that <math id="Thmproofx1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n+\ell-1\leqslant k&lt;n\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>⩽</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n+\ell-1\leqslant k&lt;n\ell</annotation></semantics></math>. Note that there exists at least one layer <math id="Thmproofx1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>z</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{z}</annotation></semantics></math> for which <math id="Thmproofx1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>’s cache always holds fewer than <math id="Thmproofx1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> pages, as <math id="Thmproofx1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k/\ell&lt;n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>/</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k/\ell&lt;n</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmproofx1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmproofx1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We define the request sequence <math id="Thmproofx1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> such that the <math id="Thmproofx1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th round contains pages <math id="Thmproofx1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{1}^{(j)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{1}^{(j)}</annotation></semantics></math> for each <math id="Thmproofx1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\neq z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\neq z</annotation></semantics></math> and page <math id="Thmproofx1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{i\pmod{n}+1}^{(z)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mspace style="width:0.949em;" width="0.949em"></mspace><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">mod</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{i\pmod{n}+1}^{(z)}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Here, each layer contributes one page, except <math id="Thmproofx1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>z</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{z}</annotation></semantics></math>, which cycles through <math id="Thmproofx1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> pages.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmproofx1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmproofx1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Since <math id="Thmproofx1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> cannot hold <math id="Thmproofx1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> pages of <math id="Thmproofx1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>z</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{z}</annotation></semantics></math>, it incurs at least one cache miss every <math id="Thmproofx1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> rounds (or <math id="Thmproofx1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\ell</annotation></semantics></math> requests). An optimal algorithm, if <math id="Thmproofx1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k&gt;n+\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k&gt;n+\ell</annotation></semantics></math>, can store all requested pages, ensuring no cache miss after initially filling the cache.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmproofx1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmproofx1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Thus, <math id="Thmproofx1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> has an unbounded competitive ratio.
∎</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The following theorem provides a lower bound on the competitive ratio of any deterministic algorithm. The result shows that the best competitive ratio cannot be much smaller than that of standard online paging, namely <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 3.2</span></span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For every value of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math>, every deterministic layered paging algorithm has a competitive ratio
at least <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k-\ell+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k-\ell+1</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmproofx2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmproofx2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof</span></span></h6>
<div id="Thmproofx2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmproofx2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Consider a deterministic layered paging algorithm <math id="Thmproofx2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>, and let <math id="Thmproofx2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=n\ell-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=n\ell-1</annotation></semantics></math>. We define a sequence <math id="Thmproofx2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> such that two consecutive rounds differ in exactly one request: the page <math id="Thmproofx2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{i}^{(j)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{i}^{(j)}</annotation></semantics></math> missing from <math id="Thmproofx2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>’s cache at the start of the round is requested and the other requests are repeated from the previous round. The first round contains arbitrary valid requests.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmproofx2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmproofx2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The algorithm <math id="Thmproofx2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> suffers one cache miss at every round by construction. Consider a round <math id="Thmproofx2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> where an optimal solution <span id="Thmproofx2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Opt</span> suffers a cache miss. <span id="Thmproofx2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Opt</span> evicts the page which is requested the latest. There are <math id="Thmproofx2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math> pages requested during round <math id="Thmproofx2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>, so at most <math id="Thmproofx2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> pages requested between rounds <math id="Thmproofx2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmproofx2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i+k-\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i+k-\ell</annotation></semantics></math>, included. The next cache miss of <span id="Thmproofx2.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Opt</span> is at round <math id="Thmproofx2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i+k-\ell+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i+k-\ell+1</annotation></semantics></math> at the earliest, so <span id="Thmproofx2.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Opt</span> suffers at most one cache miss every <math id="Thmproofx2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k-\ell+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k-\ell+1</annotation></semantics></math> rounds. Therefore, <math id="Thmproofx2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A(\sigma)\geqslant(k-\ell+1)\textsc{Opt}(\sigma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⩾</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Opt</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A(\sigma)\geqslant(k-\ell+1)\textsc{Opt}(\sigma)</annotation></semantics></math>, so <math id="Thmproofx2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\CR(A)\geqslant k-\ell+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">C</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⩾</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\CR(A)\geqslant k-\ell+1</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">LRU</span> is <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-competitive for layered paging, since it is <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-competitive in standard paging, but does it achieve a better competitive ratio for <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math>-layered paging? The following theorem answers in the negative, for any <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 3.3</span></span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For any <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math> divides <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k+1</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\CR(\textsc{LRU})\geqslant k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">C</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">r</mi></mrow><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">LRU</mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⩾</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\CR(\textsc{LRU})\geqslant k</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmproofx3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmproofx3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof</span></span></h6>
<div id="Thmproofx3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmproofx3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="Thmproofx3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=(k+1)/\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=(k+1)/\ell</annotation></semantics></math>. We build a sequence <math id="Thmproofx3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> that cycles through all <math id="Thmproofx3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\ell=k+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\ell=k+1</annotation></semantics></math> pages, respecting the layer constraints, achieving the worst case for <span id="Thmproofx3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">LRU</span> compared to <span id="Thmproofx3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Opt</span>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmproofx3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_minipage" style="width:138.0pt;">
<table id="S3.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{array}[c]{ccc}\sigma=(&amp;p_{1}^{(1)},p_{1}^{(2)},\ldots,p_{1}^{(\ell)}&amp;\\
&amp;p_{2}^{(1)},p_{2}^{(2)},\ldots,p_{2}^{(\ell)}&amp;\\
&amp;\ldots&amp;\\
&amp;p_{n}^{(1)},p_{n}^{(2)},\ldots,p_{n}^{(\ell)}&amp;\\
&amp;p_{1}^{(1)},p_{1}^{(2)},\ldots,p_{1}^{(\ell)}&amp;\\
&amp;\ldots&amp;).\end{array}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo fence="true" lspace="0em">CLOSE</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd><mrow><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd><mrow><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd><mrow><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd><mrow><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mtd><mtd><mrow><mrow><mo fence="true" rspace="0em">OPEN</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{array}[c]{ccc}\sigma=(&amp;p_{1}^{(1)},p_{1}^{(2)},\ldots,p_{1}^{(\ell)}&amp;\\
&amp;p_{2}^{(1)},p_{2}^{(2)},\ldots,p_{2}^{(\ell)}&amp;\\
&amp;\ldots&amp;\\
&amp;p_{n}^{(1)},p_{n}^{(2)},\ldots,p_{n}^{(\ell)}&amp;\\
&amp;p_{1}^{(1)},p_{1}^{(2)},\ldots,p_{1}^{(\ell)}&amp;\\
&amp;\ldots&amp;).\end{array}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
