<section id="S5.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.3 </span>Shared vs. Distributed Cache</h3>

<div id="S5.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This second set of experiments aims at exploring the performance gap between shared and unified cache systems. We saw in <a href="#S3" title="3 Competitive Analysis of Layered Paging ‣ Cache Management for Mixture-of-Experts LLMs – extended version" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> that an algorithm that splits the cache can have an arbitrarily big competitive ratio, which we want to test in practice. In <a href="#S5.F6.sf1" title="In Figure 6 ‣ 5.3 Shared vs. Distributed Cache ‣ 5 Performance Evaluation through Simulations ‣ Cache Management for Mixture-of-Experts LLMs – extended version" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6(a)</span></a>, we compare the number of faults of <span id="S5.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Opt</span> and <span id="S5.SS3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Opt-Dist</span> using a synthetic trace generated with a Zipf distribution, for a range of values of <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math> and a fixed cache of size <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>. We observe that for the values for which everything fits in the cache, the ratio is <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>, but whenever this is not possible anymore, <span id="S5.SS3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_smallcaps">Opt-Dist</span> makes significantly more cache faults, up to <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="3\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3\times</annotation></semantics></math> more than <span id="S5.SS3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_smallcaps">Opt</span>. Using some reasonable values for an MoE system (<math id="S5.SS3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="32" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>32</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">32</annotation></semantics></math> layers, <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>8</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">8</annotation></semantics></math> experts per layer and <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="64" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>64</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">64</annotation></semantics></math> experts fitting in the cache), we show in <a href="#S5.F6.sf2" title="In Figure 6 ‣ 5.3 Shared vs. Distributed Cache ‣ 5 Performance Evaluation through Simulations ‣ Cache Management for Mixture-of-Experts LLMs – extended version" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6(b)</span></a> how the <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> parameter of the underlying Zipf law affects the performances. We observe two effects: when <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> is very big, the data disparity is such that almost always the same one expert will be chosen at each layer, hence both <span id="S5.SS3.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Opt</span> and <span id="S5.SS3.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Opt-Dist</span> achieve similar results. Whenever <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> gets smaller, page disparity between layers is still present, and <span id="S5.SS3.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Opt-Dist</span> starts underperforming, with up to <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m14" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="1.8\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.8</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1.8\times</annotation></semantics></math> more page faults. More importantly, whenever <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> gets close to <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e. there is no rank imbalance, <span id="S5.SS3.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Opt-Dist</span> still performs poorly. This further solidifies the theoretical findings of <a href="#S3" title="3 Competitive Analysis of Layered Paging ‣ Cache Management for Mixture-of-Experts LLMs – extended version" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> and real life experiments of <a href="#S5.SS2" title="5.2 Results on MoE Traces ‣ 5 Performance Evaluation through Simulations ‣ Cache Management for Mixture-of-Experts LLMs – extended version" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a> which states that split caching is sub-optimal and should not be used in practice.</p>
</div>
